---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout
  title="Frequency Separation & Liquify — Varve Docs"
  description="Use Varve's source-preserving Frequency Separation and Liquify workflows for tone, texture, and raster deformation."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Docs</a> / Tools / Retouching
      </nav>

      <h1>Retouch tone, texture, and shape with pixels you can still edit.</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Frequency Separation and Liquify share Varve's raster pipeline. They keep the source pixels
        authoritative, put authored changes in the document model, and make the limits visible.
        Nothing in these workflows needs a model download or a cloud connection.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="frequency-title">
        <h2 id="frequency-title">Frequency Separation</h2>
        <p>
          Select a raster layer and choose <strong>Frequency Separation</strong> from the command
          palette. The dialog shows <strong>Before</strong>, <strong>Combined</strong>,
          <strong>Tone</strong>, and <strong>Detail</strong> views before anything is committed.
          Radius is Gaussian sigma in layer pixels, so zoom, device-pixel ratio, preview size, and
          export resolution do not change the authored split.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Review the measured reconstruction error and choose the radius that matches the material's texture scale.</li>
          <li>Apply to create a linked group containing ordinary <strong>Tone</strong> and <strong>Detail</strong> raster layers.</li>
          <li>Retouch the selected component with paint, clone, heal, blur, masks, or effects. A detail value of 128 is neutral; it means “leave this texture unchanged,” not “paint gray into the photograph.”</li>
          <li>Choose the group and run the command again to re-split the current composite at a different radius. Existing edits are read first and are not silently thrown away.</li>
          <li>Use <strong>Flatten Frequency Separation</strong> only when you intentionally want one raster layer.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The current implementation is an 8-bit sRGB two-band split. RGB residuals are signed
          internally and stored with a neutral 128 encoding; alpha remains source coverage and is
          not added to the residual equation. Reconstruction is bounded to at most one channel
          step for an unedited split. Gaussian is the supported method today; nonlinear median,
          bilateral, and multiscale wavelet variants are not presented as if they had the same
          reconstruction contract.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="liquify-title">
        <h2 id="liquify-title">Liquify</h2>
        <p>
          Select one editable raster and press <strong>Y</strong>, or choose Liquify from the Photo
          tools. The options panel shows the actual target, brush mode, size, strength, falloff,
          pressure mapping, protection, and grid controls.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Push</strong> follows the pointer; Expand, Contract, Twirl, Restore, and Smooth are local deformation operations.</li>
          <li>Pointer samples are coalesced in order, predictions are preview-only, and the release sample is committed once. A stationary Push does not drift from event-rate or timer changes.</li>
          <li>The field is output-to-source and the original raster remains the sampling source. Previewing, undoing, re-entering, and exporting do not repeatedly warp an already warped bitmap.</li>
          <li>Freeze and Thaw are raster protection controls. A Frequency Separation group can receive a shared deformation, but group-level freeze is explicitly unavailable; select Tone or Detail for an advanced component-only field.</li>
          <li>Reset clears the visible target's field. Resetting a selected band does not unexpectedly remove its sibling's deformation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Vector, text, and arbitrary groups are not silently rasterized by Liquify. Select a raster
          target or use the explicit guidance in the panel. Geometry-level vector liquify remains a
          separate capability because it needs curve adaptation, hit testing, and export semantics.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="combined-title">
        <h2 id="combined-title">Using both tools</h2>
        <p>
          The order matters because nonlinear spatial deformation and frequency decomposition do
          not commute. Liquify first when the shape correction should be the source for a new split.
          Retouch the linked Tone/Detail group first when the deformation should move the finished
          composite. Deforming only one band is intentionally marked as an advanced operation and
          can create a different result than a shared group deformation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The group marker links stable node IDs rather than layer names. Renaming, duplication,
          save/reopen, undo, and export preserve that relationship; a missing or malformed link
          falls back to ordinary group rendering instead of decoding unrelated pixels.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="review-title">
        <h2 id="review-title">Save, reopen, and export</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Commit the stroke or separation and wait for the normal <strong>Saved</strong> state.</li>
          <li>Reload or reopen the document from Home. The Tone/Detail IDs, residual tiles, deformation field, and protection mask are document data, not a temporary canvas or blob URL.</li>
          <li>Export from the normal Export panel. PNG and other raster outputs use the committed source plus field at the requested resolution, not the viewport preview.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Inspect the written file when delivery matters. An export event alone does not prove that
          a deformed or separated result was encoded. SVG/PDF retain unaffected vector and text
          structure; a raster dependency is rasterized only where the target format cannot represent
          the effect.
        </p>
      </section>

      <aside class="docs-callout">
        <strong>Further reading</strong>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath('/features/retouching')}>Retouching feature overview</a> ·
          <a href="https://github.com/K-Arthur/varve/blob/main/docs/architecture/frequency-separation-liquify.md">engine contract and verification</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/export')}>Export &amp; Code Generation</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 860px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .docs-page h1 { max-width: 760px; margin-bottom: 1rem; font: 700 clamp(2.3rem, 6vw, 4rem)/1.05 var(--font-editorial); color: var(--text-primary); letter-spacing: -.025em; }
  .docs-intro { max-width: 720px; }

  .docs-section ol, .docs-section ul { padding-left: 1.4rem; }
  .docs-section a, .docs-callout a { color: var(--text-link); }
  .docs-callout { border-left: 3px solid var(--border-accent); padding: 1rem 1.25rem; background: var(--surface-accent-soft); color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .docs-callout p { margin: .5rem 0 0; line-height: 1.7; }
</style>
